yesterday i talked generally about  the white house press corps apparent  inability or lack of desire to ask  serious questions of white house press  secretary jen saki  we talked yesterday about the dogs and  cats  questions from the previous day today i  want to drill into  a two-minute exchange between jen saki  and fox news reporter peter ducey  now i'm going to admit up front because  i think it's more interesting  if i'm honest with you there is a kernel  of a good topic here that peter tries to  bring up  but he's so blinded by his desperation  to be hysterical and histrionic uh and  and make the exchange as confrontational  and dramatic as possible  that what could be an interesting topic  gets lost in the noise of hyperbole  i won't say more let's just hear the  question and then we'll talk about it  cdc tells these migrant shelter  facilities  that they can be at full capacity if  they are careful about covid  many of them do but when the cdc tells  schools  that they can open in person at full  capacity many of them don't  is there a school in particular that you  have as an example that didn't  do that are most schools in this country  at full capacity with  in-person learning so the question in  general  is fair but he asks it in a completely  dishonest deceptive and needlessly  dramatic way  he says how can the same cdc be saying  child  migrant detention facilities at the  border can be open at full capacity  but schools can't open at full capacity  because it's too dangerous  the fair part of this question is that  in both schools and migrant centers  both have the potential to have a lot of  kids crowded together  and we have a pandemic why can one be  open and the other one can't  that's a perfectly fair kernel of a  question  now the circumstances of course are  completely different kids in schools  most of them have parents most of the  possibility of remote learning  not all i understand there's  communication between schools and  parents and kids  there's an apparatus where the  relationship is completely different  the migrant children at the border you  can't say  we're going to do remote border  facilities where you will be with your  parents and via zoom you will join the  border facility like it doesn't even  compute it doesn't make any sense  the circumstances are completely  different in both cases  the cdc has put out guidelines and  recommendations  the reason the guidelines aren't the  same is because the scenarios are  completely different and what's possible  is completely different  but peter is desperate to have these  gotcha moments and so what is actually a  good topic  gets lost to the hyperbole and  histrionic questions  let's continue with it are is there a  specific school though that is not  following the cdc guidelines of  of implementing the mitigation steps so  they can reopen  the cdc is saying schools you can be at  every school can be at full capacity  the cdc guideline the cdc guidelines  just to be clear because i think this is  very important to be very clear and  specific on  they they gave eight mitigation steps  that schools can take  to safely reopen a number of schools  have actually recently  reopened schools in washington d.c some  have schools in many districts across  the country  but each school district needs to make  the decision about whether they are  able to take those mitigation steps the  president has also been clear  some of these school districts need  additional funding there's 160 billion  dollars  in this package that he's going to sign  into law later this week secretary of  education will be quite focused on  working with school districts to  help them reopen but more school  districts are reopening  more kids are in classrooms every single  day so jenn saki points out  lots of schools aren't open because they  haven't met the guidelines  some schools haven't met the guidelines  because they can't afford to meet the  guidelines without the coveted relief  money  which was finally passed yesterday so  many schools  are soon going to have the money to do  what the cdc recommends  and to get open like that's substantive  that's that's  important this is separate from  what makes sense now you know we we've  talked about  does it make sense or not to open  schools does the immigration policy make  sense or not  i just want to focus on the hypocrisy of  peter ducey  that's created when in reality these are  very different  circumstances he's blinded by his  unquenchable thirst for drama  maybe his desire to impress his dad  who's also a fox news host but  uh he he keeps going and then he changes  his approach a little bit  but since they are not all back from an  administration  position or from your perspective have  the border patrol  unions and the hhs unions been easier to  work with than  the teachers unions i i think that's a  little bit of mixing  different circumstances i would say that  it's children  all in tight quarters uh  i mean like classroom but not quite  funny  not quite so jen saki's opinion  of whether it's easier to work with  border patrol or teachers unions is  really irrelevant  but eventually peter gets to how he  should have started  which is that both the border migrant  centers and the schools are children in  tight quarters that's true  that's the similarity what is different  such that we have different guidelines  that's a fair starting point  it's not the way he went about it here's  just the last few moments of this  i i would say that let's let's take a  responsible approach to the two issues  okay  one is schools reopening there's been  eight mitigation steps that have been  announced by the cdc to implement right  every school district is going to work  to implement those  on a timeline that is uh they can  effectively do many school districts are  reopening right  many are reopening every single week and  day and week  right that is a different circumstance  than what we are seeing at the border so  bottom line  peter has a kernel of relevance wrapped  in a veneer of gotcha and gensake  often handles parts of it well and then  gets a little flustered for other parts  she's she's mostly good at appearing  calm even if we get the sense that she's  frustrated she does  sort of have to be like the patient mom  when the kids are misbehaving  but the important part is that this is  the culture war  sort of framing where the context for  everything is  this is hostility towards law  enforcement  and deference to teachers unions for  example like that's that's one  cultural element of why this is framed  in such hyperbolic terms  or it's a purported left-wing double  standard when the truth is  listen circumstances are different in a  school versus  in a child migrant facility and peter  ducey will likely keep doing this for as  long as fox news keeps him in the  briefing room because my  my instinct is the fox news viewer sees  this  and says look at that he's holding jen  saki to account  and jensaki is doing a terrible job of  answering these questions that's my  instinct of how the right  sees peter ducey's participation in this  and to the extent that they like it  i would expect if places like oan and  newsmax can get back in the press  briefing room  i would expect them to do this but  scaled up like 10 notches  today's show is sponsored by an  excellent audiobook  called takeover how a conservative  student club  captured the supreme court this is the  latest audiobook from noah feldman it  just came out  and feldman actually narrates the  audiobook himself  noah feldman of course is a harvard law  professor and constitutional  scholar you may remember him from his  prominent role in trump's impeachment  number one  and the book takeover looks at how the  federalist society became the most  powerful legal organization in the  country  six of the nine current supreme court  justices  are current or former members of the  federalist society  and takeover dives into what the  organization is what it stands for  its relationship with american politics  the federalist  society is an organization anyone in my  audience  should be aware of and the audiobook has  a ton of excellent insight into it and  the role it plays i really enjoyed  listening to the audio book i think  anybody  in my audience would as well so grab  takeover by noah feldman the audiobook  is just 499  and you can get it at davidpakman.com  slash takeover or wherever you buy  audio books
 

